// Practice quiz: Cleaning and validating records with Pydantic and type hints
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. First draft, not reviewed. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/data-pipelines/cleaning-and-validating-records-with-pydantic-and-type-hints

::o1 required::Which field declaration is required? {
=sku\: str #Correct. No default means the key must be present.
~note\: str | None \= None #Not quite. The default of None makes it optional.
~active\: bool \= True #Not quite. A default makes it optional.
~qty\: int \= 0 #Not quite. A default makes it optional.
}

::o1 hints at runtime::A plain function declares def f(qty\: int) and is called with f("two"). Without Pydantic, what happens at the call? {
=Python runs it; plain type hints are not enforced at runtime #Correct. Enforcement is what Pydantic adds.
~A TypeError is raised at the call #Not quite. The runtime does not check annotations.
~The string converts to an int #Not quite. Nothing converts it without a library.
~A ValidationError is raised #Not quite. That comes from Pydantic models, not plain functions.
}

::o2 loc::In the error details of a ValidationError, what does loc tell you? {
=Which field failed #Correct. loc is the path to the failing value, such as the field name.
~Which line of the input file failed #Not quite. Pydantic knows nothing about your file; record that yourself.
~Where in memory the model lives #Not quite. It describes the input location.
~Which Python module raised it #Not quite. It points into the data, not the code.
}

::o2 bare except::Why catch ValidationError rather than using a bare except in a validation loop? {
=A bare except also hides your own bugs, such as a misspelled name #Correct. Catch only what you expect, so real defects still surface.
~A bare except is a syntax error #Not quite. It is legal, just unwise.
~ValidationError cannot be caught any other way #Not quite. A broad except would catch it too, along with everything else.
~It makes validation faster #Not quite. Speed is not the reason.
}

::o3 int to str::A model declares sku\: str and receives the int 42 in default mode. What happens? {
=ValidationError with type string_type #Correct. Lax mode does not convert an int to a str.
~sku becomes the string "42" #Not quite. That conversion is not applied by default in Pydantic 2.
~sku stays the int 42 #Not quite. The value fails validation instead.
~A TypeError from Python #Not quite. Pydantic reports it as a ValidationError.
}
